SELF-TEST
  (a) magnitude-only fit of a known min-phase net recovers its PHASE
      to 0.00 deg  ->  OK
  (b) excess-phase monotonicity: a rise is impossible for a causal element
      min-phase              tails    +0/   +0 dB/oct   max rise    +0.6 deg  (clean)
      min-phase              tails   +12/  -12 dB/oct   max rise   +26.6 deg  (flagged)
      + all-pass (legal)     tails    +0/   +0 dB/oct   max rise    -1.8 deg  (clean)
      + all-pass (legal)     tails   +12/  -12 dB/oct   max rise   +24.6 deg  (flagged)
      + INVERSE all-pass     tails    +0/   +0 dB/oct   max rise   +12.6 deg  (flagged)
      + INVERSE all-pass     tails   +12/  -12 dB/oct   max rise   +36.0 deg  (flagged)
      -> with TRUE tails only the INVERSE all-pass is flagged (an all-pass
         itself makes the excess FALL, which is legal); with guessed tails
         everything is flagged. So the absolute test
         is only as good as the tails, and the branch comparison below is
         used instead: branch M minus branch P is 2.theta_ped, which does
         not involve min_phase at all and is therefore TAIL-FREE.

